{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>A soft answer turns away wrath, and a grievous word stirs up anger (Proverbs 15:1).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, admonishes in this verse (Proverbs 15) to every person to elevate his soul and to habituate his nature and his tongue to a soft answer, for the soft answer will quiet and calm the anger of the angry one, and the grievous words, which are the opposite of a soft answer, will produce anger and wrath." "<b>And a word.</b> Know that speech has great power, both for good and for its opposite, and it is the essence of man's existence, like the fruit which is the essence of the tree. From this the verse says (Genesis 2:7): ״ויהי האדם לנפש חיה.״ Its meaning is that the essence of man's existence is for the sake of a living soul, which is the speaking soul. And so Onkelos translated: לרוח ממללא — 'a speaking spirit.' And therefore speech is called 'fruit,' as it is written (Isaiah 57:19): ״בורא ניב שפתים״ — 'creating the fruit of the lips,' for speech is the fruit of the lips. And the reason the verse attributes speech to the Creator, may He be exalted, is because speech emanates from the power of the intellect, which is from the side of the speaking soul in man; for beasts, which have no intellect, have no speech; and with speech man has superiority over the rest of the living creatures, as it is written (Job 35:11): ״מלפנו מבהמות ארץ ומעוף השמים יחכמנו״." "<b>And there are</b> in speech two ways: the way of life and the way of death, as it is written (Proverbs 18:21): ״מות וחיים ביד לשון ואוהביה יאכל פריה.״ And this is an admonition to one who is a master of words — whether in words of Torah and matters of the commandments, which is the way of life, or in evil tongue and the other transgressions dependent on speech, which is the way of death. And Solomon the king, peace be upon him, came and said that according to the abundance of his words in the good way, his fruit will be holy, dedicated to YHWH, and he will have much good reward; and according to the abundance of his words in the evil way, his fruit will be bitter and evil and he will have much punishment. And because speech is a great principle in man, to save his soul and body or to destroy them, Solomon came and taught the people knowledge, that they should hold fast to this trait of a soft answer, for it turns away wrath — even the wrath of the king, as it is written (Proverbs 16:14): ״חמת מלך מלאכי מות״." "<b>And behold,</b> Judah son of Jacob held fast to this trait, for he spoke to Joseph in a soft tongue, and therefore he turned away his wrath — which he had been showing them, that he was angry over the matter of the goblet." "<b>Know</b> that there was no fitting cause for anger against Yosef, who was framing them in the matter of the goblet, but only against Yehudah and the rest of his brothers, the innocent ones, who were making their defense and were not equal to him. Even so, Yehudah was a great sage and mighty, both in physical might and in strength of soul, for he conquered his inclination and did not become angry, even though it would have been fitting for them to be angry. Yehudah saw that now there was no place for anger, but rather there was need for a soft reply, in order to turn back the wrath of the master Yosef. Therefore he approached him to contend with him, and he wished to review all the matters that had befallen them with him until now, and he aimed at three things: for judgment, for appeasement, and for war. And this is what is written:" "<b>Then Yehudah approached him and said: 'Please, my lord, let your servant speak a word in my lord's ears, and let your anger not burn against your servant, for you are like Pharaoh.'</b> We find that the language of approaching (הגשה) serves for three things: judgment, appeasement, and war. Judgment — as it is written (Deuteronomy 25:1): ״ונגשו אל המשפט ושפטום״. Appeasement — as it is written (Joshua 14:6): ״ויגשו בני יהודה אל יהושע״. War — as it is said (II Samuel 10:13): ״ויגש יואב והעם אשר עמו למלחמה״. Now Yehudah intended all three of them, and from this he said 'Please, my lord' (בי אדני), as one who comes with his weapon of war in his hand, arrayed for battle, and says: 'In me (בי) you will find everything you desire, whether for judgment, whether for appeasement, whether for war.'" "<b>Or</b> he says 'Please, my lord' (בי אדני): 'put it upon me' (העון בי), for the guilt of the goblet was found with Binyamin, and Yehudah and his brothers have no guilt in this matter that they sinned, save for Binyamin alone. Therefore he entreats him, to say to him that he should not place the guilt upon Binyamin but upon himself, and when he detains him it is as though the goblet was found in his own hand, and that he should release Binyamin — for this is the request of Yehudah throughout all the account of his words, and it is the thing that he was seeking; concerning it he said, 'Let your servant speak a word' (ידבר נא עבדך דבר), for this word is the substitution of Binyamin for himself." "<b>And let your anger not burn against your servant.</b> That is to say: when I say to release Binyamin, the suspected one, and to seize me, the innocent one — I have a claim in this, for a man like you, a prince and commander of nations like Pharaoh, it is fitting for you to hear my arguments and the reason for which I say such a thing to you, to seize the innocent one and to release the suspected one. And he proceeds to enumerate his arguments, for this he does on account of the pledge of suretyship that he made concerning him, and he begins to recall the earlier matters: 'My lord asked his servants.'" "<b>Let your servant speak a word.</b> He sought permission from him, that he might speak before him, and he began to speak in soft language, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 25:15): ״בארך אפים יפתה קצין ולשון רכה תשבר גרם״. And it is written (Proverbs 15:1): ״מענה רך ישיב חמה״. And the words of Yehudah in this parashah are words of contention, in which he contends with Yosef, and through the wisdom of Yehudah he wished now to review some of the matters that had befallen them with him, in order to stir his compassion and so that he would be appeased toward him through the abundance of his supplications, for he thought that he was a fearer of Elohim, as he had mentioned to them (Genesis 42:18–19): ״את האלהים אני ירא״. And therefore he said:" "<b>My lord asked his servants, saying.</b> That is to say: not of ourselves but under compulsion did we report concerning this our brother, because of the questioning of my lord; nor did we volunteer to bring him down before you as your first command — rather we said that the lad cannot leave his father. But because you said, ״You shall not see my face again,״ we were forced to bring him with our very lives on account of the fury of the famine, and our father was forced to send him with us against his will with great anxiety, and now upon seeing that — the lad would die of a bitter soul." "<b>And your servants brought down.</b> An expression he used instead of saying 'and you brought down.' Or one may say והורידו — 'they brought him down' — for they are the cause. Therefore let my plea fall before you, that you show grace to us and have compassion on us and on the old man, and take me in place of the lad as a permanent slave, for I am better than him in every respect, and for you it will be righteousness, and so you will be doing several kindnesses: you will acquire a slave better than him, and you will sustain the soul of the old man, and you will save me from sin, for there is no remedy without this." "<b>And now, let your servant remain instead of the lad as a slave to my lord… for how shall I go up to my father.</b> That is to say: it is better for me to be a permanent slave than to go up to my father without the lad." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ויגש אליו יהודה — the lion stood against the ox. Judah is called a lion, as it is said (Genesis 49:9): ״גור אריה יהודה״; Joseph is called an ox, as it is said (Deuteronomy 33:17): ״בכור שורו הדר לו״. ויאמר בי אדני — do not bring upon us the attribute of justice. ידבר נא עבדך דבר באזני אדני — let my words enter your ears. This one's grandfather — because Pharaoh detained him one night, he and his household were stricken with great plagues, as it is said (Genesis 12:17): ״וינגע ה׳ את פרעה״ וגו׳ — beware lest you be stricken. This one's mother did not die except by his father's curse, as it is said (Genesis 31:32): ״עם אשר תמצא את אלהיך לא יחיה״ — beware lest a curse fall upon you. Two of his entered the city of Shechem and destroyed it on account of a female; here it is on account of a male — all the more so concerning the inn of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Deuteronomy 33:12): ״חופף עליו כל היום״. כי כמוך כפרעה — just as Pharaoh decrees and does not fulfill, so now you decree and do not fulfill. And is this what you meant by the 'setting of the eye' you spoke of: ״ואשימה עיני עליו״? Just as Pharaoh lusts after males, so you lust after males. Just as Pharaoh is king and you are second to him, so my father is king in the land and I am second to him. And if I draw my sword — from you I begin and with Pharaoh your master I finish."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:44.345441637Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}